# saphira-agents: developer/agent workstation

One command makes a fresh Saphira machine an agent-development box:

    apk add saphira-agents
    saphira-agents --setup          # per-user scope (default)
    sudo saphira-agents --setup     # machine-wide scope

Later:

    saphira-agents --list           # installable catalogue
    saphira-agents --installed      # audit what this tool recorded
    saphira-agents --update         # refresh recorded packages

What the package carries:

- /usr/bin/saphira-agents: the interactive installer for coding
  agents (OpenCode v2, Codex, Claude Code) and model provider SDKs.
  Per-user installs land under ~/.local; global installs (root)
  land in /usr, root-owned, executable by all users.
- Dependencies only otherwise: nodejs, rtk, grit, bubblewrap. No
  configs, hooks, services or home-directory files.

What it deliberately does not carry:

- Coding agents and provider SDKs as APKs: that ecosystem moves too
  fast for APK rebuilds. The installer fetches them explicitly at
  operator request via npm, vendor-direct (hash-verified) and pip
  backends, with per-package lifecycle policy
  (narrow --allow-scripts approvals only, never blanket). "Latest"
  means newest signed Saphira package for the APK layer, and newest
  upstream release explicitly chosen here for the npm layer -- never
  a runtime download hidden inside an install.
- Credentials, logins or provider configuration: system executables
  are shared machine-wide; auth and sessions stay per-user.

Node.js invariant: the runtime comes from the Saphira nodejs APK
only (bare `nodejs` dependency, so apk upgrade tracks new Saphira
Node revisions normally; nodejs22 is never pulled in
automatically). The installer consumes /usr/bin/node and
/usr/bin/npm owned by that APK and installs npm application
packages only -- never the Node/npm runtime itself, no version
managers, no runtime replacement.

saphira-ai stays reserved for a future broader AI umbrella (local
inference, speech, models) and is not used here.
